{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "p......."}, {"name": "s_valid", "wave": "01......"}, {"name": "s_data", "wave": "x====.==", "data": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F"]}, {"name": "m_ready", "wave": "1..01..."}, {"name": "s_ready", "wave": "1...01.."}, {"name": "skid", "wave": "x...=x..", "data": ["C"]}, {"name": "m_data", "wave": "x.==.===", "data": ["A", "B", "B", "C", "D"]}], "head": {"text": "m_ready drops: C goes into the skid slot, s_ready drops a cycle later, nothing is lost"}}
```

### Explanation
- Two storage slots (output register + skid register); `s_ready` depends only on a flop -> no combinational path from m_ready to s_ready.
- Throughput stays one item per cycle when m_ready is high.
- Also called a register slice (AXI) or elastic buffer.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Only the valid flags need reset; data registers can stay un-reset. Verify with randomized m_ready (including long stalls) and an in-order scoreboard.

### Follow-ups
- *Why not just register ready?* Without the skid slot, the item sent during the late-ready cycle would be lost.

**Key idea:** An interface bundles related signals (plus modports, clocking blocks, tasks and assertions) and can be parameterized for widths/options, giving a reusable connection for both RTL and testbench.

```systemverilog
interface axis_if #(parameter int DW = 32, parameter bit HAS_LAST = 1) (input logic clk, rst_n);
  logic          tvalid, tready;
  logic [DW-1:0] tdata;
  logic          tlast;

  modport src (output tvalid, tdata, tlast, input tready, clk, rst_n);
  modport snk (input  tvalid, tdata, tlast, output tready, input clk, rst_n);

  clocking drv_cb @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step output #1;
    output tvalid, tdata, tlast; input tready;
  endclocking

  a_hold: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
            tvalid && !tready |=> tvalid && $stable(tdata));
endinterface

axis_if #(.DW(64)) s_axis (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n));
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  SRC["producer (modport src)"] --> IF["axis_if #(DW=64)"] --> SNK["consumer (modport snk)"]
  TB["UVM driver (virtual axis_if)"] -.-> IF
```

### Explanation
- Parameters must match between the interface instance and the virtual interface type in the TB (`virtual axis_if #(64)`).
- Embedding protocol assertions in the interface checks every instance automatically.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Parameterized virtual interfaces make UVM components parameterized too; a common workaround is using max width or an abstract class (polymorphic interface).

### Follow-ups
- *Can interfaces be synthesized?* Yes, flattened to signals by synthesis.

**Key idea:** A modport defines a directional view of an interface for a particular user (master, slave, monitor), so the compiler can check that a module only drives what it should.

```systemverilog
interface bus_if (input logic clk);
  logic req, gnt;
  logic [31:0] addr;
  modport master  (input clk, gnt, output req, addr);
  modport slave   (input clk, req, addr, output gnt);
  modport monitor (input clk, req, gnt, addr);
endinterface

module cpu (bus_if.master bus);
  always_ff @(posedge bus.clk) bus.req <= 1'b1;   // legal
  // assign bus.gnt = 1'b1;                       // compile error: gnt is input
endmodule
```

| Modport | req | gnt | addr |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| master | output | input | output |
| slave | input | output | input |
| monitor | input | input | input |

### Explanation
- Modports can also export/import tasks and functions and include clocking blocks (`modport tb (clocking cb)`).
- Selecting the modport can be done in the module header or at instantiation (`cpu u_cpu(.bus(b.master))`).

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without a modport, all signals are inout-like and direction errors go undetected.

### Follow-ups
- *Virtual interface with modport?* `virtual bus_if.master vif;` restricts the class view as well.

**Key idea:** A virtual interface is a variable in a class that holds a handle to a real (static) interface instance, letting dynamic class-based TB components (drivers, monitors) access DUT signals without hard-coded hierarchical paths. In UVM it is passed through `uvm_config_db`.

```systemverilog
// top module
module tb_top;
  bit clk;
  axis_if vif_inst (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n));
  dut u_dut (.s_axis(vif_inst));
  initial begin
    uvm_config_db#(virtual axis_if)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agt*", "vif", vif_inst);
    run_test();
  end
endmodule

// driver
class axis_driver extends uvm_driver #(axis_item);
  virtual axis_if vif;
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual axis_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "virtual interface not set")
  endfunction
endclass
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  TOP["tb_top: axis_if instance"] -->|config_db set| CDB["uvm_config_db"]
  CDB -->|get in build_phase| DRV["driver.vif"]
  CDB -->|get| MON["monitor.vif"]
  DRV --> PINS["DUT pins via vif"]
```

### Explanation
- Classes cannot contain static interface instances; virtual interfaces bridge static and dynamic worlds.
- The same component can be reused for multiple instances by pointing it to different interfaces.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Null virtual interface at run time -> fatal; always check the `get` return value.

### Follow-ups
- *Abstract class alternative?* 'Polymorphic interface': an abstract API class implemented inside the interface.

**Key idea:** A clocking block groups signals synchronous to a clock and defines when the TB samples them (input skew, default #1step = just before the edge) and when it drives them (output skew after the edge). The TB thus never reads or writes at the exact same instant the DUT does.

```systemverilog
interface bus_if (input logic clk);
  logic req, gnt;
  clocking cb @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step output #1ns;
    output req;
    input  gnt;
  endclocking
  modport tb (clocking cb);
endinterface

// in driver
@(vif.cb);                 // wait for clocking event
vif.cb.req <= 1'b1;        // driven 1ns after the edge
if (vif.cb.gnt) ...        // value sampled 1step before the edge
```

```wavedrom
{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "0.1...0.1..."}, {"name": "gnt (DUT)", "wave": "0..1........"}, {"name": "cb.gnt sampled", "wave": "0.......1..."}, {"name": "cb.req driven", "wave": "0..1........"}], "head": {"text": "input sampled just before the edge (#1step); output driven after it (#1ns)"}}
```

### Explanation
- Clocking-block drives are applied in the Re-NBA region; sampling uses Preponed values, so DUT and TB orders don't matter.
- `##N` delays count clocking events of the default clocking block.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Mixing direct signal writes (`vif.req = 1`) with clocking-block drives on the same signal causes confusing behavior.

### Follow-ups
- *Input skew #0?* Samples in Observed region (after design settles) - can see same-edge DUT updates.
